{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target is one-sided: it makes sky-view geometry the single dominant mechanism for both the existence and cross-city variation of the nocturnal UHI, omitting anthropogenic heat release and surface thermal storage as alternative dominant contributors. But the problem asks what single mechanism BEST explains the gap; by excluding anthropogenic heat from its claim/mechanism while the counterconditions do not guard against that rival, it fails to justify why radiative trapping is superior.","counterexample":["Urban heating studies (e.g., Oke 1982; Sailor 2011) find anthropogenic heat emissions and high thermal-admittance building materials substantially raise nocturnal urban temperatures, so cross-city differences in energy use and material heat capacity—not primarily sky-view factor—can dominate the UHI gap; the target omits this and offers no evidence that geometry dominates."],"premise":"The problem asks for the single best mechanism, so an answer asserting sky-view geometry must show why that dominates plausible rivals like anthropogenic heat release and stored thermal admittance, which are well-established contributors to nocturnal UHI.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"4dd62735b90864a75220e09b302500bc","quote":"what single mechanism best explains why that night-time gap is larger in some cities than in others?"}],"target":"71d69d64a2c38f681be55e6c81531049cf621f16eac98864cc5aa84c512136de"}]}